CVE-2017-7525 (databind#1599, initial DEFAULT_NO_DESER_CLASS_NAMES blacklist) -> CVE-2018-5968 (databind#1899,
jackson-databind 2.8.11.1 / 2.7.9.5 / 2.9.5). CWE-502 + CWE-184 (incomplete List of Disallowed Inputs). Sink:
default-typing polymorphic deserialization (ObjectMapper.enableDefaultTyping(); readValue(body, Object.class))
-> BeanDeserializerFactory.createBeanDeserializer -> SubTypeValidator.validateSubType() over
DEFAULT_NO_DESER_CLASS_NAMES (default-ALLOW enumerated class-name blacklist -> incomplete). CVE-2017-7525
(#1599) + CVE-2017-15095 (#1680/#1737) + CVE-2017-17485 (#1855) built up the enumeration; CVE-2018-5968
(#1899) adds two MORE gadget class names ("// [databind#1899]: more 3rd party"):
  org.hibernate.jmx.StatisticsService
  org.apache.ibatis.datasource.jndi.JndiDataSourceFactory
Neither is in the V1 (2.8.11) blacklist; both are added in V2 (2.8.11.1) -- confirmed empirically by the gate
below.

ARCHITECTURE (real-app-http):
- Per profile a REAL small Java HTTP REST service (com.pcb.DeserHttpService, JDK built-in
  com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpServer, maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-8) boots with the REAL, UNMODIFIED published
  com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind:{2.8.8,2.8.11,2.8.11.1} (+ transitive jackson-core /
  jackson-annotations, pulled from Maven Central at build via `mvn dependency:copy-dependencies
  -Djackson.version=${JACKSON_VER}`) on its classpath. It exposes POST /deser (Content-Type application/json)
  -> om.enableDefaultTyping(); Object o = om.readValue(requestBody, Object.class); i.e. the real polymorphic
  deser of an attacker JSON body in default-typing array form ["<FQCN>", {}]. jackson-databind is NOT modified
  (runs_real_software=true, is_verbatim_lift=false).
- The differential is fired by REAL external HTTP (external_http_trigger=true, run_mode=real-app-http): the
  oracle POSTs the benign default-typing JSON body to http://127.0.0.1:{18780,18781,18782}/deser.
- BENIGN SHADOW-CLASS canary (NO gadget): harmless empty Serializable beans occupy the exact blacklisted FQCNs
  on the service classpath (canary/): org.apache.commons.collections.functors.InvokerTransformer (ORIGINAL, a
  7525/#1599 name present in BOTH 2.8.11 and 2.8.11.1); org.hibernate.jmx.StatisticsService and
  org.apache.ibatis.datasource.jndi.JndiDataSourceFactory (RESIDUAL, the two #1899 names added only in
  2.8.11.1). The real gadget libs (commons-collections, hibernate-core, mybatis) are NOT on the classpath, so
  the blacklist's name-based decision -- not any real gadget -- governs. Each shadow bean's no-arg CONSTRUCTOR
  reads the planted /canary/flag.txt and stashes the token in com.pcb.CanaryHolder (a per-thread marker); the
  HTTP service echoes PCB_CANARY_FLAG=<token> in the response iff a shadow bean was actually instantiated past
  the blacklist. If validateSubType() blocks the name, the ctor never runs and no flag is emitted.

